Toward a Universal Dynamical Map of Human Physiology

A Rigorous Framework for Reconstructing Population-Level Transition Laws from Age-Stratified, Multiscale Longitudinal Observations

Author: Ouadi Maakoul
Version: v0.4 (Final Pre‑Experimental Draft)
Status: Theoretical research proposal; no experiments, physical tests, or code training have been conducted.

---

Abstract

The central scientific question of this work is whether the laws governing human physiological transitions can be inferred from sufficiently rich longitudinal observations. We answer this question conditionally: given explicit bounds on the observability deficit, the effective dimension of the physiological manifold, and the age‑period‑cohort non‑identifiability, consistent estimation of population‑level transition laws is possible from age‑stratified, short‑duration longitudinal cohorts. The core mathematical contribution is the observability deficit, a quantitative measure of the dynamical relevance of unobservable state directions, and a theorem that decomposes the total estimation error into four components: curse of dimensionality, state estimation error, observability deficit, and measurement noise. We show that nonparametric estimation without structural assumptions is infeasible for realistic conditioning dimensions, and we outline the structural assumptions (manifold hypothesis, sparsity, additivity) required for tractability. The framework is positioned as a formal analysis of existing infrastructure (iPOP, Human Phenotype Project, EDITH, Physiome), not a proposal for new measurement technology. A concrete pilot study is proposed to map the observability deficit in glucose‑HRV dynamics using existing data. This version contains no experimental results; all claims are mathematical or conceptual.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 The Multiscale Nature of Human Physiology

Human physiology is a multiscale dynamical system with characteristic timescales spanning nine orders of magnitude, from cardiac (~1 s) to aging (~10⁹ s). The Physiome Project (Hunter & Borg, 2003) established that any unified model must integrate across these scales. Recent infrastructure—longitudinal multi‑omics profiling (Chen et al., 2012), population‑scale multimodal phenotyping (Shilo et al., 2025), the European Virtual Human Twin (EDITH, 2024), and generative models such as HealthFormer (Shilo et al., 2026) and GluFormer (Lutsker et al., 2024)—has demonstrated the feasibility of collecting and modeling physiological trajectories at scale. What remains missing is a rigorous mathematical treatment of when and how such heterogeneous, age‑stratified, short‑duration observations can be combined to identify population‑level physiological transition laws.

1.2 Prior Art and Positioning

This work explicitly builds upon four pillars:

1. Longitudinal multi‑omics profiling: iPOP demonstrated the integration of genomic, transcriptomic, proteomic, and wearable data over 14 months (Chen et al., 2012).
2. Population‑scale phenotyping: The Human Phenotype Project (HPP) has collected continuous glucose, wearables, and multi‑omics from ~28,000 participants (Shilo et al., 2025). HealthFormer (Shilo et al., 2026) implements a generative model of physiological trajectories, and GluFormer (Lutsker et al., 2024) predicts glucose and health outcomes from CGM data.
3. Virtual twin infrastructure: EDITH (European Commission, 2024) provides a federated repository and governance architecture.
4. Theoretical foundations: Villaverde et al. (2016, 2019) developed structural identifiability and observability analysis for biological ODEs; the critical slowing down literature (Scheffer et al., 2009) provides early‑warning signals for loss of resilience; the age‑period‑cohort (APC) literature (Fosse & Winship, 2019) establishes the fundamental non‑identifiability of age effects in cross‑sectional data.

The contribution of this framework is not new measurement infrastructure. It is a formal analysis of the statistical and dynamical conditions under which existing data can recover the conditional transition law:

P\left(X(t+\Delta t) \mid X(t), \tau, E, G, U\right),

where X(t)\in\mathbb{R}^n is the latent physiological state, \tau is chronological age, E environmental context, G genetic variation, and U perturbations.

1.3 Central Scientific Question

Can the laws governing human physiological transitions be inferred from sufficiently rich longitudinal observations?

More precisely, we ask under what conditions the above transition law is identifiable from heterogeneous observations collected across age‑stratified cohorts, given the known limitations of observability, the APC collinearity, and the curse of dimensionality.

