3. Latent State Representation and the Observability Deficit

3.1 Physiological State Space

We model the latent physiological state X(t)\in\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^n as a compact, smooth manifold with boundary (Assumption: bounded biological state). The ambient dimension n_{\text{full}} is large (possibly 10^4–10^6), but we hypothesize that the effective dimension d of the manifold on which human physiology actually operates is much smaller (Hypothesis: physiological manifold, d \ll n_{\text{full}}). This manifold hypothesis is common in physiological time series analysis (Glass, 2001) and is the basis for dimensionality reduction.

3.2 Observation Model

Each sensing modality k observes a projection of the latent state:

Y_k(t) = h_k(X(t)) + \epsilon_k(t),

where h_k:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{m_k} and \epsilon_k(t) is measurement noise (bounded variance, unbiased). The combined observation operator H = [h_1^T, \dots, h_K^T]^T maps \mathcal{X} to \mathbb{R}^{m}, with m = \sum_k m_k. Critically, Y(t) \neq X(t).

3.3 Observability Deficit: A Quantitative Criterion

The classic observability rank condition (Hermann & Krener, 1977) requires that the Lie derivatives of H along f span the tangent space at every point. In practice, this condition often fails partially: there exist directions in state space that are unobservable but may still be dynamically relevant.

Definition 3.1 (Observability Deficit). For a system \{\dot{X} = f(X), Y = H(X)\} at state x\in\mathcal{X}, define

\delta_O(x) = \min_{\substack{v \in \ker(dH(x)) \\ \|v\| = 1}} \|\nabla_v f(x)\|,

where \ker(dH(x)) is the null space of the Jacobian of H, and \nabla_v f(x) is the directional derivative of f along v.

Interpretation. \delta_O(x) measures the maximum dynamical relevance of an unobservable direction:

· If \delta_O(x) = 0, there exists an unobservable direction that is dynamically inert; it can be safely ignored.
· If \delta_O(x) > 0, there exists an unobservable direction along which the dynamics are nontrivial. The larger \delta_O(x), the more the unobservable subspace matters for prediction, and the less reliable any estimate of the transition law will be.
· If \delta_O(x) \gg \|f(x)\|, the unobservable subspace dominates the dynamics, and the framework cannot provide reliable estimates.

The observability deficit is local and can be estimated from data once f and H are known (e.g., from a trained neural SDE). It provides an operational criterion for deciding when new sensing modalities are required.

