7. Falsifiable Predictions and Validation Strategy

We propose six predictions, each with explicit falsification criteria.

Prediction 1

High‑frequency physiological trajectories contain information about future states not recoverable from isolated measurements.

Test: Compare predictive accuracy (e.g., next‑day glucose) of trajectory‑based vs. snapshot‑based models on held‑out HPP data. Falsified if ΔAUC < 0.05.

Prediction 2

Combining physiological and molecular trajectories improves prediction versus either alone.

Test: Multimodal model vs. single‑modality on same dataset. Falsified if no significant improvement.

Prediction 3

Some dynamical features of healthy physiology are approximately invariant across populations after adjustment.

Test: Estimate f in HPP and All of Us; test invariance. Falsified if dynamics differ systematically.

Prediction 4

Dynamic resilience measures provide information beyond static biomarkers.

Test: Association between resilience (recovery rate) and incident disease. Falsified if no incremental predictive value.

Prediction 5

Trajectory features distinguish individuals with similar chronological age but different biological states.

Test: Cluster by trajectory features; test if clusters predict health outcomes beyond age. Falsified if no added value.

Prediction 6 (Expanded)

Pathological transitions produce detectable changes in trajectory dynamics before clinical thresholds are crossed (critical slowing down).

Pre‑registration protocol:

· Population: HPP participants who develop type 2 diabetes during follow‑up (incident cases).
· Controls: matched by age, BMI, sex, baseline HbA1c, who do not develop diabetes.
· Signal: Increasing variance and autocorrelation of glucose‑HRV coupling over time.
· Test: Time‑dependent AUC comparing early‑warning score vs. standard HbA1c screening.
· Falsification threshold: If the trajectory‑based score does not achieve at least 0.05 improvement in AUC at 6 months before diagnosis, the prediction is falsified.

