Appendix A: Detailed Mathematical Proofs and Derivations

A.1 Proof of Proposition 2.1 (Information Monotonicity)

Proposition. For a non-degenerate dynamical system with additive noise, the information contained in a trajectory segment  X([0,T])  strictly exceeds the information contained in any finite collection of instantaneous measurements  \{X(t_1), \ldots, X(t_m)\}  with  t_j \in [0,T] .

Proof. Let  \Theta  be a finite-dimensional parameter vector governing the dynamics (e.g., parameters of  f ). The Fisher information matrix for the full trajectory  X([0,T])  is:

I_{\text{traj}}(\Theta) = \mathbb{E}\left[ \left( \frac{\partial \log p(X([0,T]) \mid \Theta)}{\partial \Theta} \right) \left( \frac{\partial \log p(X([0,T]) \mid \Theta)}{\partial \Theta} \right)^\top \right].

For any finite set of time points  \{t_1, \ldots, t_m\} , the Fisher information of the corresponding observations  \{X(t_1), \ldots, X(t_m)\}  is  I_{\text{snap}}(\Theta) . By the data processing inequality for Fisher information (Zamir, 1998), since  \{X(t_1), \ldots, X(t_m)\}  is a deterministic function of the full trajectory (evaluation maps), we have:

I_{\text{snap}}(\Theta) \preceq I_{\text{traj}}(\Theta),

where  \preceq  denotes the Loewner order on positive semidefinite matrices. For non-degenerate dynamics, the inequality is strict because the trajectory contains derivative information not present in finite snapshots. \square

---

A.2 Proof of Proposition 2.2 (Velocity Information)

Proposition. Under regularity conditions, the pair  (X(t_0), \dot{X}(t_0))  provides first-order information about the transition dynamics  f , while  X(t_0)  alone provides none without additional assumptions.

Proof. From the SDE  \dot{X}(t) = f(X(t), U(t), E(t), \tau(t), G) + \eta(t) , at time  t_0  we have  \dot{X}(t_0) = f(X(t_0), \cdot) + \eta(t_0) . Given only  X(t_0) , the value  f(X(t_0), \cdot)  is unconstrained: any drift function passing through  X(t_0)  is consistent. Given both  X(t_0)  and  \dot{X}(t_0) , and assuming knowledge of the noise distribution (e.g., zero mean), we obtain the constraint  f(X(t_0), \cdot) = \dot{X}(t_0) - \eta(t_0) , which provides direct information about the local drift. \square

---

A.3 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Age-Period-Cohort Collinearity)

Theorem. Let  \tau  denote chronological age,  \pi  calendar period, and  c  birth cohort. These three variables are perfectly collinear:  \tau = \pi - c . Any linear model of the form  Y = \alpha \tau + \beta \pi + \gamma c + \varepsilon  is non-identifiable.

Proof. Substituting  \tau = \pi - c  yields  Y = \alpha(\pi - c) + \beta \pi + \gamma c + \varepsilon = (\alpha + \beta)\pi + (\gamma - \alpha)c + \varepsilon . For any  \lambda \in \mathbb{R} , define  \alpha' = \alpha + \lambda ,  \beta' = \beta - \lambda ,  \gamma' = \gamma + \lambda . Then  \alpha' + \beta' = \alpha + \beta  and  \gamma' - \alpha' = \gamma - \alpha , so the model predictions are identical. Hence infinitely many parameter triples yield the same likelihood. \square

---

A.4 Observability Rank Condition (Theorem 9.2)

Theorem (Hermann & Krener, 1977). For the system  \dot{X} = f(X) ,  Y = H(X) , local observability at  x_0  holds if the observability matrix  \mathcal{O}(x)  (constructed from Lie derivatives of  H  along  f ) has full rank  n  at  x_0 .

Proof. This is a standard result in nonlinear control theory. The Lie derivatives generate the observability codistribution; if it spans the cotangent space at each point, the mapping from initial state to output trajectory is locally injective. The proof uses the inverse function theorem on the Taylor series expansion of the output. \square

---

A.5 Proof Sketch of Theorem 4.2 (Error Decomposition)

Theorem (Main Error Bound). Under assumptions A1–A10, the estimator  \hat{P}_N  satisfies

\mathbb{E}\left[\|\hat{P}_N - P\|^2\right] \le C_1 N^{-2/(2+d_Z^{\text{eff}})} + C_2 r_N^2 + C_3 \delta_{\max}^2 + C_4 \sigma_\epsilon^2.

Detailed Proof Sketch.

1. Trajectory existence and uniqueness. By A1 (Lipschitz drift) and A2 (compact state space), the SDE has a unique strong solution (Øksendal, 2003). This ensures that the transition law  P(X_{t+\Delta t} \mid X_t, Z)  is well-defined.
2. Distinguishability under bounded observability deficit. Define the identifiable region  \Omega_{\text{id}} = \{x \in \Omega : \delta_O(x) \le \delta_{\max}\} . For any two distinct states  x_1, x_2 \in \Omega_{\text{id}} , the output distributions differ in the  L^2  sense with a lower bound proportional to  \|x_1 - x_2\|  times a factor depending on  \delta_{\max} . This follows from the definition of  \delta_O : if  x_1 - x_2  lies partly in the null space of  dH , then the difference in observations is attenuated, but the unobservable component has bounded dynamical effect. Hence state estimation is possible with bias bounded by  C\delta_{\max} .
3. State estimation error. Using a consistent filter (e.g., particle filter under A9), we obtain estimates  \hat{X}(t)  with mean squared error  r_N^2 , where  r_N \to 0  as  N \to \infty  and as measurement noise decreases. The rate depends on the filtering method (see Appendix D).
4. Transition law estimation with errors-in-variables correction. The naive kernel regression using  \hat{X}  is biased. Applying a deconvolution kernel or simulation-based inference (Appendix E) yields a corrected estimator whose bias due to state estimation error is of order  r_N^2 . The remaining variance is that of nonparametric regression in the effective dimension  d_Z^{\text{eff}} , which gives the first term  C_1 N^{-2/(2+d_Z^{\text{eff}})} .
5. Bias from observability deficit. Even with infinite data, if  \delta_{\max} > 0 , the transition law is not identifiable in the unobservable directions. The resulting bias is bounded by  C_3 \delta_{\max}^2 , where  C_3  depends on the Lipschitz constant of  f  and the geometry of  \Omega .
6. Measurement noise. The irreducible noise variance  \sigma_\epsilon^2  propagates into the transition law estimate through the observation likelihood, contributing a bias term  C_4 \sigma_\epsilon^2  if not corrected. Deconvolution can reduce but not eliminate this.

Combining these terms via triangle inequality and the bias-variance decomposition yields the stated bound. \square

