A.6 Proof of Proposition 12.2 (Resilience and Lyapunov Exponents)

Proposition. For a linear system  \dot{R} = A R  with  A  Hurwitz stable, the resilience metric  \rho = \frac{\|X(t_0)\|}{\|X(t_0)\| + \sqrt{\int_{t_0}^\infty \|R(s)\|^2 ds}}  equals

\rho = \frac{\|X(t_0)\|}{\|X(t_0)\| + \sqrt{R(t_0)^\top W_c R(t_0)}},

where  W_c = \int_0^\infty e^{A^\top s} e^{A s} ds  is the controllability Gramian of the pair  (A, I) .

Proof. For linear system,  R(s) = e^{A(s-t_0)} R(t_0) . Then

\int_{t_0}^\infty \|R(s)\|^2 ds = \int_0^\infty R(t_0)^\top e^{A^\top s} e^{A s} R(t_0) ds = R(t_0)^\top W_c R(t_0).

Substituting into the definition yields the result. The eigenvalues of  A  (Lyapunov exponents) determine the decay of  e^{A s} ; more negative eigenvalues make  W_c  smaller and resilience higher. \square

