Appendix B: Observability Deficit – Theory and Computation

B.1 Formal Definition and Properties

Definition B.1. For system  \dot{x} = f(x) ,  y = H(x) , define at  x \in \mathcal{X} :

\delta_O(x) = \min_{\substack{v \in \ker(dH(x)) \\ \|v\|=1}} \|\nabla_v f(x)\|.

If  \ker(dH(x)) = \{0\} , set  \delta_O(x) = 0 .

Properties:

1.  \delta_O(x) \ge 0 .
2.  \delta_O(x) = 0  iff either  \ker(dH(x)) = \{0\}  (full observability) or there exists an unobservable direction along which  f  is constant (dynamically inert).
3.  \delta_O(x)  is invariant under smooth coordinate transformations.
4. If  H  is extended by adding new sensors (i.e.,  H  replaced by  [H; h_{\text{new}}] ), then  \ker(dH)  shrinks, and  \delta_O(x)  decreases or stays the same.

B.2 Relationship to Empirical Observability Gramian

The empirical observability Gramian  W_o^{\text{emp}}  (Singh & Hahn, 2005) measures global observability over a time horizon. The local deficit  \delta_O(x)  complements it:  W_o^{\text{emp}}  positive definite implies local observability almost everywhere, but  \delta_O(x)  pinpoints regions where unobservable directions have high dynamical relevance.

B.3 Computational Approach Using Neural SDEs

Given a trained neural SDE model  \dot{x} = f_\theta(x) + \sigma_\theta(x) \xi ,  y = h_\phi(x) + \epsilon , we can compute  \delta_O(x)  by:

1. Evaluate Jacobians  J_f = \frac{\partial f_\theta}{\partial x} ,  J_h = \frac{\partial h_\phi}{\partial x}  via automatic differentiation.
2. Compute null space of  J_h  (e.g., via SVD). If null space dimension is zero,  \delta_O = 0 .
3. For each basis vector  v_i  of the null space, compute  \|\nabla_{v_i} f\| = \|J_f v_i\| . Then  \delta_O = \min_i \|J_f v_i\| .

B.4 Worked Example: 2D Linear System

Consider  \dot{x}_1 = -a x_1 + b x_2 ,  \dot{x}_2 = -c x_2 , and observation  y = x_1 . Then  H = [1, 0] , so  dH = [1, 0]^\top . Null space of  dH  is spanned by  v = [0, 1]^\top . The directional derivative  \nabla_v f = J_f v = \begin{bmatrix} -a & b \\ 0 & -c \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{bmatrix} = \begin{bmatrix} b \\ -c \end{bmatrix} . Thus  \delta_O = \sqrt{b^2 + c^2} . If  b  (coupling from unobserved to observed) is large, unobservability is problematic; if  b=0 , the unobserved variable does not influence the observed one and the deficit is just  |c|  (the unobserved variable's own decay), but that is dynamically irrelevant for the observed trajectory.

