Appendix C: Age-Period-Cohort Analysis in Detail

C.1 Formal Statement of Non-Identifiability

The APC model for a scalar outcome  Y  is  Y_{ijk} = \mu + \alpha_i + \beta_j + \gamma_k + \varepsilon_{ijk} , with  i  indexing age,  j  period,  k  cohort, and  k = j - i . The design matrix is rank deficient by 1. The null space is spanned by the vector  \lambda(1, -1, 1) , so  \alpha_i + \lambda ,  \beta_j - \lambda ,  \gamma_k + \lambda  give identical fits.

C.2 Solutions: Within-Individual Change and Survival Bias

Within-individual change  \Delta_{\text{within}}(\tau)  is identifiable because it uses only temporal variation within individuals of the same cohort. However, survivors may be a biased subset. Define survival indicator  S(t) = \mathbb{1}[T_{\text{death}} > t] . The observed within-individual change among survivors is:

\Delta_{\text{within}}^{\text{obs}}(\tau) = \mathbb{E}[X(t+\Delta t) - X(t) \mid \tau(t) = \tau, S(t+\Delta t)=1].

This differs from the full population counterfactual  \Delta_{\text{within}}^{\text{full}}(\tau)  if mortality is selective. We propose joint modeling of  X(t)  and  S(t)  using shared random effects or inverse probability weighting (IPW) with weights  w_i = 1/P(S_i=1 \mid X_i, Z_i) .

C.3 Joint Modeling Approaches

Let  h_i(t) = h_0(t) \exp(\beta^\top X_i(t) + \gamma^\top Z_i)  be the hazard for death. The joint likelihood for longitudinal trajectory  Y_i  and survival outcome  (T_i, \delta_i)  is:

L_i = \int p(Y_i \mid X_i) \, p(X_i) \, p(T_i, \delta_i \mid X_i) \, dX_i.

This can be fitted using Bayesian methods (e.g., MCMC) or EM algorithm. The resulting posterior for  \Delta_{\text{within}}^{\text{full}}(\tau)  accounts for survival bias.

C.4 Sensitivity Analysis Framework

We propose a pattern-mixture approach: assume different relationships between missing data (due to death) and trajectory. For each pattern, estimate  \Delta_{\text{within}}(\tau)  and report the range of results across plausible missingness mechanisms (e.g., missing at random, missing not at random with monotonic or non-monotonic effect).

