Appendix D: State Estimation and Filtering Methods

D.1 Extended Kalman Filter (EKF)

For nonlinear dynamics with additive Gaussian noise:

· Prediction:  \hat{x}_{t|t-1} = \hat{x}_{t-1|t-1} + \int_{t-1}^t f(\hat{x}_{s|t-1}) ds ,  P_{t|t-1} = F_t P_{t-1|t-1} F_t^\top + Q_t , where  F_t = \exp(J_f \Delta t) .
· Update:  K_t = P_{t|t-1} H_t^\top (H_t P_{t|t-1} H_t^\top + R_t)^{-1} ,  \hat{x}_{t|t} = \hat{x}_{t|t-1} + K_t (y_t - h(\hat{x}_{t|t-1})) ,  P_{t|t} = (I - K_t H_t) P_{t|t-1} .

Works well for mildly nonlinear systems; may diverge for strong nonlinearity.

D.2 Unscented Kalman Filter (UKF)

Uses sigma points to propagate mean and covariance through nonlinear functions without linearization. More accurate than EKF for moderate nonlinearity; computational cost similar.

D.3 Particle Filter (Sequential Monte Carlo)

Represents posterior by weighted samples  \{x_t^{(i)}, w_t^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N . Prediction: sample from transition density. Update:  w_t^{(i)} \propto w_{t-1}^{(i)} p(y_t \mid x_t^{(i)}) . Resample when effective sample size is low. Asymptotically exact as  N \to \infty , but suffers from degeneracy in high dimensions. Convergence rate  r_N \sim N^{-1/2}  under mixing conditions (Crisan & Doucet, 2002).

D.4 Ensemble Kalman Filter (EnKF)

Maintains an ensemble of states; update uses sample covariance. Assumes Gaussianity but handles high dimensions well. Rate  N^{-1/2}  under Gaussian assumptions.

D.5 Neural SDE / Variational Inference

Parameterize drift  f_\theta  and diffusion  \sigma_\theta  as neural networks. Use a variational posterior  q_\phi(X_{0:T} \mid Y_{0:T})  (e.g., a recognition network). Maximize the evidence lower bound (ELBO):

\mathcal{L}(\theta, \phi) = \mathbb{E}_{q_\phi}\left[ \sum_{t} \log p_\theta(y_t \mid x_t) - \text{KL}(q_\phi(x_{0:T}) \| p_\theta(x_{0:T})) \right].

This yields a probabilistic state estimator and model simultaneously. Convergence rates for variational methods are an active research area; typically slower than particle filters but scalable to high dimensions.

D.6 Convergence Rates and Conditions

· Particle filter: Under strong mixing and bounded likelihood,  \mathbb{E}\|\hat{x}_t - x_t\|^2 = O(N^{-1})  for fixed time  t .
· EnKF: For linear Gaussian systems, exact; for nonlinear, bias may persist.
· Variational inference: May be biased but consistent if variational family is rich enough.

The choice of  r_N  in the main theorem depends on the method used.

---

Appendix E: Errors-in-Variables Correction Methods

E.1 Deconvolution Kernel Regression

Assume we observe  \hat{X}_i = X_i + \xi_i  where  \xi_i \sim p_\xi  known (e.g., Gaussian with variance  \tau^2 ). The true transition density  p(x_{t+\Delta t} \mid x_t)  is convolved with  p_\xi . A deconvolution kernel  K_h^*  is chosen such that  \int K_h^*(u) e^{i \omega u} du = \frac{\phi_K(h\omega)}{\phi_\xi(\omega)} , where  \phi_K  is the Fourier transform of the base kernel and  \phi_\xi  is the characteristic function of  \xi . Then the estimator:

\hat{p}_N(x_{t+\Delta t} \mid x_t) = \frac{\sum_i K_h^*(x_t - \hat{X}_i(t)) K_h^*(x_{t+\Delta t} - \hat{X}_i(t+\Delta t))}{\sum_i K_h^*(x_t - \hat{X}_i(t))}

is consistent if  \phi_\xi  has no zeros (e.g., Gaussian). Rate is typically slower than the no-error case; the bias is reduced.

E.2 Simulation-Based Inference (SBI)

Use the filtering posterior  p(X_t^i \mid Y_i(0:t))  as a proposal. The transition law estimator becomes:

\hat{P}_N^{\text{SBI}}(A \mid x_t, Z) = \frac{\sum_i \int \int \mathbb{1}[x_{t+\Delta t} \in A] K_h(x_t - X_t^i) p(X_{t+\Delta t}^i, X_t^i \mid Y_i) dX_{t+\Delta t}^i dX_t^i}{\sum_i \int K_h(x_t - X_t^i) p(X_t^i \mid Y_i) dX_t^i}.

This integrates over the uncertainty in the latent states. Computationally expensive; can be approximated by Monte Carlo sampling from the posterior.

E.3 Variational Approximations

Instead of full posterior, use a variational approximation  q_\phi(X_t^i \mid Y_i) . Replace the integrals with expectations under  q_\phi . This is efficient but may underestimate uncertainty, leading to residual bias.

