Appendix F: Manifold Hypothesis and Dimensionality Reduction

F.1 Formal Statement and Estimation Methods

Hypothesis F.1 (Physiological Manifold). Human physiology operates on a smooth manifold  \mathcal{M} \subset \mathcal{X}  of intrinsic dimension  d \ll n_{\text{full}} .

To estimate  d  from data, we can use:

· Maximum Likelihood Estimator (MLE) (Levina & Bickel, 2005): For each point  x_i , compute distances to its  k -nearest neighbors and use Poisson process approximation.
· Two-NN Estimator (Facco et al., 2017): Uses the ratio of distances to the first and second nearest neighbors; consistent for  d  and robust to non-uniform sampling.
· Graph-based methods: Analyze eigenvalue spectrum of graph Laplacian.

F.2 Manifold Learning Algorithms

· Isomap: Preserves geodesic distances via shortest paths in neighborhood graph.
· UMAP: Optimizes a fuzzy simplicial set representation; effective for visualization and downstream clustering.
· Diffusion Maps: Uses eigenfunctions of Markov transition matrix to embed data; robust to noise.

For high-dimensional time series, we can first reduce dimension using variational autoencoders (VAEs) with a low-dimensional bottleneck, then apply manifold learning.

F.3 Implications for Sample Size

If the effective conditioning dimension after manifold reduction is  d_Z^{\text{eff}} , then the nonparametric rate becomes  N^{-2/(2+d_Z^{\text{eff}})} . For example, if  d_Z^{\text{eff}} = 5 , achieving MSE  10^{-4}  requires  N \approx 10^{14} . This still large but more feasible with very large cohorts or parametric models.

