Appendix G: Simulation Study Design

All simulations are to be conducted before applying methods to real data.

G.1 Low-Dimensional Benchmark: van der Pol Oscillator

Model:  \dot{x}_1 = x_2 ,  \dot{x}_2 = \mu(1 - x_1^2)x_2 - x_1 + \sigma \eta(t) . Observation:  y = x_1 + \epsilon . Dimensions:  n=2 ,  m=1 . Goal: test whether the framework recovers the drift and estimates the observability deficit. Expected:  \delta_O  varies with  x_1 ; regions of high nonlinearity may have larger deficit.

G.2 Moderate-Dimensional Coupled Oscillators

Model: 10 coupled Kuramoto oscillators with sparse connectivity, additive noise. Observation: only 5 phase variables. Goal: test whether manifold learning recovers the low-dimensional effective dynamics and whether transition law estimation with errors-in-variables correction improves over naive.

G.3 High-Dimensional Linear State-Space with Low-Rank Structure

Model:  x_{t+1} = A x_t + w_t ,  y_t = C x_t + v_t , with  x_t \in \mathbb{R}^{100} ,  y_t \in \mathbb{R}^{20} ,  A  has rank 5. Goal: assess performance of state estimation (EnKF, particle filter) and transition estimation under known ground truth.

G.4 Age-Stratified Population Simulation with APC Effects

Simulate a population of individuals born in different cohorts, with age-dependent drift, cohort-specific intercept shifts, and period effects (e.g., environmental changes). Observe each individual for 1 year. Test whether the framework can separate age dynamics from cohort and period using the within-individual change approach and sensitivity analysis.

G.5 Performance Metrics and Success Criteria

· State estimation: mean squared error (MSE) between true and estimated latent states.
· Transition law estimation: integrated squared error (ISE) between true and estimated conditional density.
· Observability deficit: correlation between estimated  \delta_O  and true dynamical relevance of unobservable directions (constructed analytically).
· APC separation: bias and variance of estimated age effect when cohort and period effects are present; coverage of confidence intervals.

Success criteria: For low-dimensional systems, the framework should recover dynamics within 10% relative error; for moderate dimensions, the manifold dimension should be correctly identified; for APC, the within-individual change estimator should have lower bias than cross-sectional age trends.

