Appendix H: Pilot Study Protocol (Glucose-HRV Observability Deficit)

H.1 Data Source and Preprocessing

· Source: Human Phenotype Project (HPP) dataset, containing continuous glucose monitor (CGM) data (5-min), wearable heart rate and HRV (1-min), accelerometry (1-min), and sleep logs for ~28,000 participants.
· Subset: Participants with at least 14 consecutive days of simultaneous CGM and wearable data, no diagnosed diabetes, age 18-80. Estimated N = 500.
· Preprocessing: Align timestamps, impute missing values (e.g., linear interpolation for gaps < 30 min), normalize signals, remove artifacts using standard filters.

H.2 Latent State Model Specification

We posit an 8-dimensional latent state  X(t) = [G, I, Gl, A, H, P, S, C] , representing glucose, insulin, glucagon, autonomic tone, HRV, physical activity, sleep debt, and circadian phase. Dynamics modeled as a neural SDE:

dX_t = f_\theta(X_t, U_t) dt + \sigma_\theta(X_t) dW_t,

where  U_t  includes meal inputs (derived from CGM rises) and activity counts. Observations:

· CGM:  y_G = h_G(X) + \epsilon_G  with  h_G  selecting glucose component.
· HRV:  y_H = h_H(X) + \epsilon_H .
· Activity:  y_A = h_A(X) + \epsilon_A .

H.3 Training Procedure for Neural SDE

Use variational inference with a recognition network  q_\phi(X_{0:T} \mid Y_{0:T})  that outputs mean and covariance of a Gaussian posterior at each time step. Optimize the ELBO using stochastic gradient descent. Architecture: both  f_\theta  and recognition network are 2-layer MLPs with 128 hidden units.

H.4 Observability Deficit Computation

After training, compute Jacobians  J_f  and  J_h  at a grid of points spanning the latent state distribution. Compute null space of  J_h  via SVD. For each null space basis vector, compute  \|J_f v\| . Obtain  \delta_O(x)  as minimum over basis.

H.5 Visualization and Interpretation

Produce heatmaps of  \delta_O  over 2D projections (e.g., glucose vs. HRV, glucose vs. activity). Identify regions where  \delta_O  is high. If high in regions corresponding to postprandial states, this suggests that insulin dynamics are unobservable from available sensors, indicating need for insulin measurement.

H.6 Statistical Analysis Plan

· Report distribution of  \delta_O  across participants.
· Test whether  \delta_O  correlates with prediction error of future glucose levels using a separate held-out set.
· Compute confidence intervals for  \delta_O  via bootstrap over participants.

