Appendix J: Ethical and Governance Framework

J.1 Privacy-Preserving Computation

· Differential Privacy: Add calibrated noise to outputs:  \mathcal{M}(D) = f(D) + \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2 \Delta f^2) , where  \Delta f  is sensitivity.
· Federated Learning: Train models without centralizing raw data:  \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t - \eta \sum_i \nabla \mathcal{L}_i(\theta_t) , with local gradients.
· Secure Multiparty Computation: Enable computations on encrypted data.

Interaction with statistical estimation: Federated transition law estimation may require sharing sufficient statistics (e.g., kernel sums) rather than raw data.

J.2 Data Sovereignty and Community Engagement

Adopt the CARE Principles for Indigenous Data Governance (Collective benefit, Authority to control, Responsibility, Ethics). For any data collected from specific communities, obtain collective consent and ensure benefit-sharing. Provide mechanisms for data withdrawal and deletion.

J.3 Dual-Use Concerns and Mitigation

Physiological models could be misused for surveillance, discrimination, or military purposes. Mitigations: restrict access to de-identified aggregate data; require ethical review for any secondary use; publish only anonymized models; prohibit use in non-medical contexts in data use agreements.

J.4 Conflict of Interest Policies

· All researchers must disclose financial interests in wearable companies, pharma, or data analytics.
· Funding should prioritize public and philanthropic sources; commercial partnerships require independent oversight.
· Data and code should be open (subject to privacy) to enable independent verification.

