4. Observability Deficit Concept

```
State space X (R^n)
  |---- observed directions (span of dH)  --> measurable
  |---- unobserved directions (null space of dH) --> invisible to sensors

Observability deficit delta_O(x) = min over unit vector v in null(dH) of ||∇_v f(x)||

Interpretation:
  - delta_O = 0  --> unobservable direction is dynamically inert (safe to ignore)
  - delta_O > 0  --> unobservable direction influences future observed state
                     (blind spot; prediction error will increase)

Illustration:

        Observed           Unobserved
           y1                 y2
            ^                  ^
            |                  |
    --------+------------------+----->  X state space
            |   dH nonzero     |   dH = 0
            |                  |
        (sensor sees)     (sensor blind)
                          But if ∇_v f points into observed subspace,
                          dynamics carry information from y2 to y1 later.
                          Then delta_O > 0 and estimation is biased.
```

